On 16 Jul 2013, Jorgen Grahn wrote:
> I wasn't dragging my feet -- they generated quickly, and tended to
> summon more archons.

My first thought was that this should be impossible.  There is specific
code in the nasty selection routine that excludes spellcasters of equal or
greater sacrifice value than the summoner, with an explicit comment that
this is to stop infinite chain summoning.  So only arch-liches can summon
archons.

Looking more closely, I see a loophole.  The code selects a nasty from a
preset list.  It endlessly retries until it has one eligible under the
anti-chain rule, but doesn't retry to avoid genocided species.  If it is
genocided, then the following makemon() call will fail, triggering a
backup path that just generates an ordinary random monster.

Perhaps you used too many genocide scrolls?  Excessive genocide would
lead to this branch being used often, and then on top of that make archons
more likely in the backup path (as well as ordinary spawning) due to the
exclusion of weaker possibilities.

The specific anti-chain summoning code still helps you a bit.  The normal
path of nasty summoning specifically ignores extinction, but the backup
path respects it.  So once you've killed 120 archons, you are done with
them if there are no archliches about.

